      "text": "securelist.com /from-blackenergy-to-expetr/78937/ As in the case of Wannacry, attribution is very difficult and finding links with previously known malware is challenging.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_2",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s2"
      },
      "text": "In the case of Wannacry, Google’s Neel Mehta was able to identify a code fragment which became the most important clue in the story, and was later confirmed by further evidence, showing Wannacry as a pet project of the Lazarus group .",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_3",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s3"
      },
      "text": "To date, nobody has been able to find any significant code sharing between ExPetr/Petya and older malware.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_4",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s4"
      },
      "text": "Analyzing the Similarities At the beginning of the ExPetr outbreak, one of our team members pointed to the fact that the specific list of extensions used by ExPetr is very similar to the one used by BlackEnergy’s KillDisk ransomware from 2015 and 2016 ( Anton Cherepanov from ESET made the same observation on Twitter).",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_5",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s5"
      },
      "text": "The BlackEnergy APT is a sophisticated threat actor that is known to have used at least one zero day, coupled with destructive tools, and code geared towards attacking ICS systems.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_6",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s6"
      },
      "text": "Going back to the hunt for similarities, here’s how the targeted extensions lists looks in ExPetr and a version of a wiper used by the BE APT group in 2015:",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_7",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 1,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p1_s7"
      },
      "text": "ExPetr 2015 BlackEnergy wiper sample 3ds, .7z, .accdb, .ai, .asp, .aspx, .avhd, .back, .bak, .c, .cfg, .conf, .cpp, .cs, .ctl, .dbf, .disk, .djvu, .doc, .docx, .dwg, .eml, .fdb, .gz, .h, .hdd, .kdbx, .mail, .mdb, .msg, .nrg, .ora, .ost, .ova, .ovf, .pdf, .php, .pmf, .ppt, .pptx, .pst, .pvi, .py, .pyc, .rar, .rtf, .sln, .sql, .tar, .vbox, .vbs, .vcb, .vdi, .vfd, .vmc, .vmdk, .vmsd, .vmx, .vsdx, .vsv, .work, .xls",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_10",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 2,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p2_s1"
      },
      "text": "Together, we tried to build a list of features that we could use to make a YARA rule to detect both ExPetr and BlackEnergy wipers.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_17",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s2"
      },
      "text": "When run on our extensive (read: very big) malware collection, the YARA rule above fires on BlackEnergy and ExPetr samples only .",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_21",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "body",
        "sentence_id": "p4_s6"
      },
      "text": "Looking back at other high profile cases, such as the Bangladesh Bank Heist or Wannacry, there were few facts linking them to the Lazarus group .",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.95,
      "note": "narrative sentence evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_28",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 3,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p3_r4"
      },
      "text": "//shutdown.exe /r /f",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_43",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p4_r4"
      },
      "text": "When run on our extensive (read: very big) malware collection, the YARA rule above fires on BlackEnergy and ExPetr samples only . Unsurprisingly, when used alone, each string can generate false positives or catch other unrelated malware. However, when combined together in this fashion, they become very precise . The technique of grouping generic or popular strings together into unique combinations is one of the most effective methods for writing powerful Yara rules.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_44",
      "evidence_type": "report_text",
      "report_id": "Kaspersky-From-BlackEnergy-to-ExPetr(06-30-2017)",
      "locator": {
        "page": 4,
        "section": "rule",
        "sentence_id": "p4_r5"
      },
      "text": "This low confidence but persistent hunch is what motivates us to ask other researchers around the world to join us in investigating these similarities and attempt to discover more facts about the origin of ExPetr/Petya. Looking back at other high profile cases, such as the Bangladesh Bank Heist or Wannacry, there were few facts linking them to the Lazarus group . In time, more evidence appeared and allowed us, and others, to link them together with high confidence. Further research can be crucial to connecting the dots, or, disproving these theories.",
      "language": "en",
      "confidence": 0.98,
      "note": "rule or indicator snippet evidence",
      "source": "document"
    },
